22:20:02  CALL    save_note({'content': '- **Definition**: Artificial Intelligence (AI) is the field of computer science dedicated to creating systems that can perform tasks that normally require human intelligence, such as perception, reasoning, learning, and decision‑making.\n\n- **Historical milestones**\n  - 1950: Alan Turing proposes the Turing Test.\n  - 1956: Dartmouth Workshop coins the term “Artificial Intelligence.”\n  - 1970s–80s: Expert systems (e.g., MYCIN) demonstrate rule‑based reasoning.\n  - 1997: IBM Deep\xa0Blue defeats world chess champion Garry Kasparov.\n  - 2012: Deep learning breakthrough with AlexNet wins ImageNet competition.\n  - 2018–2023: Large language models (GPT‑3, GPT‑4, PaLM, LLaMA) achieve human‑level language generation.\n\n- **Core subfields**\n  - Machine Learning (ML): algorithms that improve from data.\n  - Deep Learning: neural networks with many layers.\n  - Natural Language Processing (NLP).\n  - Computer Vision.\n  - Robotics.\n  - Knowledge Representation & Reasoning.\n  - AI Safety & Ethics.\n\n- **Key algorithms & models**\n  - Linear/Logistic regression, decision trees, SVMs, k‑NN.\n  - Neural architectures: CNNs, RNNs/LSTMs, Transformers.\n  - Reinforcement learning: Q‑learning, policy gradients, DQNs.\n  - Probabilistic models: Bayesian networks, MDPs.\n  - Generative models: GANs, VAEs, diffusion models.\n\n- **Major applications**\n  - Healthcare: diagnosis, drug discovery, personalized treatment.\n  - Finance: fraud detection, algorithmic trading, credit scoring.\n  - Transportation: autonomous vehicles, traffic optimization.\n  - Customer service: chatbots, sentiment analysis, recommendation systems.\n  - Manufacturing: predictive maintenance, quality inspection, supply‑chain optimization.\n  - Science: protein folding (AlphaFold), climate modeling, materials discovery.\n\n- **Ethical, legal, and societal concerns**\n  - Bias and discrimination.\n  - Transparency & explainability.\n  - Data privacy.\n  - Job displacement vs. augmentation.\n  - Dual‑use risk (disinformation, deepfakes, autonomous weapons).\n  - Regulatory efforts: EU AI Act, US Executive Orders, ISO/IEC AI standards.\n\n- **Performance benchmarks**\n  - ImageNet (state‑of‑the‑art top‑1 ~88%).\n  - GLUE / SuperGLUE (GPT‑4 > 90%).\n  - Atari 2600 (human‑level with DQN).\n  - AlphaGo/AlphaZero: superhuman across Go, chess, shogi.\n\n- **Current research frontiers**\n  - Foundation models: scaling laws, emergent abilities, multimodal integration.\n  - Efficient AI: model compression, sparsity, neuromorphic hardware.\n  - Robustness: adversarial defenses, OOD detection.\n  - Alignment: value learning, safe exploration.\n  - Neurosymbolic AI.\n  - Continual / lifelong learning.\n\n- **Future outlook (2025‑2035)**\n  - Domain‑specific foundation models (legal, medical).\n  - Expanded regulatory frameworks.\n  - Progress toward general‑purpose agents.\n  - Emphasis on energy‑efficient AI.\n  - AI‑augmented scientific discovery accelerating breakthroughs.', 'filename': 'ai.md'})
